.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/chokidar/node_modules/glob-parent Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 


.PP
\fR \fP 
.SH "glob-parent"
.PP
\fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP

.PP
Extract the non-magic parent path from a glob string\&.
.SS "Usage"
.PP
.nf
var globParent = require('glob\-parent');

globParent('path/to/*\&.js'); // 'path/to'
globParent('/root/path/to/*\&.js'); // '/root/path/to'
globParent('/*\&.js'); // '/'
globParent('*\&.js'); // '\&.'
globParent('**/*\&.js'); // '\&.'
globParent('path/{to,from}'); // 'path'
globParent('path/!(to|from)'); // 'path'
globParent('path/?(to|from)'); // 'path'
globParent('path/+(to|from)'); // 'path'
globParent('path/*(to|from)'); // 'path'
globParent('path/@(to|from)'); // 'path'
globParent('path/**/*'); // 'path'

// if provided a non\-glob path, returns the nearest dir
globParent('path/foo/bar\&.js'); // 'path/foo'
globParent('path/foo/'); // 'path/foo'
globParent('path/foo'); // 'path' (see issue #3 for details)
.fi
.PP
.SS "API"
.SS "\fRglobParent(maybeGlobString, [options])\fP"
Takes a string and returns the part of the path before the glob begins\&. Be aware of Escaping rules and Limitations below\&.
.SS "options"
.PP
.nf
{
  // Disables the automatic conversion of slashes for Windows
  flipBackslashes: true
}
.fi
.PP
.SS "Escaping"
The following characters have special significance in glob patterns and must be escaped if you want them to be treated as regular path characters:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR?\fP (question mark) unless used as a path segment alone
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR*\fP (asterisk)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR|\fP (pipe)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR(\fP (opening parenthesis)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR)\fP (closing parenthesis)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR{\fP (opening curly brace)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR}\fP (closing curly brace)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[\fP (opening bracket)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR]\fP (closing bracket)
.PP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
globParent('foo/[bar]/') // 'foo'
globParent('foo/\\\\[bar]/') // 'foo/[bar]'
.fi
.PP
.SS "Limitations"
.SS "Braces & Brackets"
This library attempts a quick and imperfect method of determining which path parts have glob magic without fully parsing/lexing the pattern\&. There are some advanced use cases that can trip it up, such as nested braces where the outer pair is escaped and the inner one contains a path separator\&. If you find yourself in the unlikely circumstance of being affected by this or need to ensure higher-fidelity glob handling in your library, it is recommended that you pre-process your input with \fRexpand-braces\fP and/or \fRexpand-brackets\fP\&.
.SS "Windows"
Backslashes are not valid path separators for globs\&. If a path with backslashes is provided anyway, for simple cases, glob-parent will replace the path separator for you and return the non-glob parent path (now with forward-slashes, which are still valid as Windows path separators)\&.

.PP
This cannot be used in conjunction with escape characters\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// BAD
globParent('C:\\\\Program Files \\\\(x86\\\\)\\\\*\&.ext') // 'C:/Program Files /(x86/)'

// GOOD
globParent('C:/Program Files\\\\(x86\\\\)/*\&.ext') // 'C:/Program Files (x86)'
.fi
.PP

.PP
If you are using escape characters for a pattern without path parts (i\&.e\&. relative to \fRcwd\fP), prefix with \fR\&./\fP to avoid confusing glob-parent\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// BAD
globParent('foo \\\\[bar]') // 'foo '
globParent('foo \\\\[bar]*') // 'foo '

// GOOD
globParent('\&./foo \\\\[bar]') // 'foo [bar]'
globParent('\&./foo \\\\[bar]*') // '\&.'
.fi
.PP
